        "title": "Count factors of 2 and 5",
        "body": "Six zeros need $2^6\\cdot5^6$. In $16\\cdot34\\cdot75\\cdot21\\cdot13$ we have $16=2^4$, $34=2\\cdot17$, $75=3\\cdot5^2$: that is $2^5$ and $5^2$. So $n$ must supply at least one more 2 and four more 5s: $n=2\\cdot5^4=1250$.\n\n**Answer: (C) 1250**"
      }
